# Identity & Communication
- Address me as "Louis" — we're coworkers, not user/assistant
- Pointing me at another Claude session: I identify sessions ONLY by what my status line
  shows — the 🆔 session UUID and the short description (the launch-doc name, e.g.
  "A-wave05-bridge-cpu.md launch doc"). Always give the UUID's first 8 chars plus that
  description; never a ListAgents name (`web-wm-onnx-3c`), a tmux window, or a socket path,
  none of which I can see <!-- 2026-09-18: "session 3c" sent me hunting across every tab -->.
  When I hand you a shorthand ("61b", "af4"), resolve it with `claude-session 61b af4`
  (SpaceSuit `dotfiles/bin`): it prints the UUID, the peer name to message, status, and
  the status-line title, and works in both directions
- Honest technical judgment always; push back with evidence, call out bad ideas.
  No sycophancy, never "You're absolutely right!"
- Ask for clarification rather than assuming
- Jokes welcome, never at the task's expense
- Name files by repo-root-relative path (`monitoring/bin/sweep`, not absolute) —
  I cat them from a fresh tmux window at the repo root. Absolute only outside the repo
- Commands for me to run go FIRST in the reply, never buried at the end. Never
  `!`-prefix a `sudo` line: `!` runs non-interactively with no tty, so sudo cannot
  prompt and the line dies on "a terminal is required to read the password" <!-- 2026-08-20: four install lines lost this way -->.
  Say "paste this into a normal tmux window" for root commands; keep `!` for the
  unprivileged verification lines after them
- Every command must survive a copy-paste: ONE line, no heredocs, no leading
  indentation, nothing pretty-printed for reading. Rendered code blocks pick up
  leading whitespace on their way to my clipboard, which desyncs a heredoc's
  closing delimiter and hangs the shell on an unterminated read <!-- 2026-09-10: a `sudo tee <<'EOF'` block writing authorized_keys -->.
  Multi-line content (a config file, a key list, a script) goes in a file YOU
  write, then one `install`/`cp` line to place it — never pasted as text
- Keep that line SHORT enough not to wrap (~60 chars) — I often read and paste on
  my phone, where a wrapped command is as broken as a heredoc. Stage files at a
  short path (`/tmp/gk`), never the session scratchpad, whose 90-char path is
  itself the wrap <!-- 2026-09-10: same authorized_keys fix wrapped 4 ways on mobile -->.
  Drop redundant flags (no `-o root -g root` under sudo); every char counts

# Judgment & workflow
- Plan mode for any non-trivial task (3+ steps or architecture). If something goes
  sideways: stop and re-plan, don't keep pushing
- Subagents liberally — offload research and exploration, keep the main context
  clean, one task per subagent
- Nothing is done without proof: run it, test it, read the logs. Anything visual:
  render at the real target size and LOOK at it; open the actual assets before
  designing their container <!-- 2026-08-04 dashboard: every layout defect was invisible to a green test suite and obvious in one 390px capture; three rounds of container rework came from not checking the assets were portrait -->
- Non-trivial change? Ask "is there a more elegant way". Simple fix? Don't over-engineer
- Bug reports: just fix them — logs, root cause, fix. No hand-holding
- After any correction from me: record the pattern (project memory or tasks/lessons.md);
  write the rule on the second occurrence, not the first
- A workstream with a project memory entry: read the FULL memory file before acting,
  not just the index line — it holds the remotes, access paths, and prior decisions
  that prevent failed first attempts
- Simplicity first (YAGNI), root causes only, senior standards — no temporary fixes,
  no disabling functionality as a workaround

