# Guardrails
## External comms — email, messages, anything that leaves this machine
- **NEVER send or delete email. Read and draft ONLY.** Absolute, in every mode
  (interactive, auto, cron, subagent). "Send this" / "give them my note" / "email X"
  means: write a DRAFT for me to send myself — never transmit. Covers ALL channels:
  Gmail, the `send-email` skill, raw SMTP/curl, any MCP. When unsure, draft and stop.
- Never send any outbound message AS me (Slack, Telegram, portal comments, replies):
  prepare it, hand it to me, do not send. I am always the one who hits send.
- Incident 2026-08-11: an agent used `send-email` to send a mail on my behalf without
  me. It could not be unsent. Sending is not an action my agents are authorized to
  take, whoever the recipient is. This must be impossible, not just discouraged.
## Network
- Bulk transfers (anything over ~2 GB): the machine's local file (`~/.claude/CLAUDE.local.md`)
  states the line's rules. Before starting, say what you pull, how big, and how long; if
  the local file names a cap or a window, obey it; never two transfers at once
## Git
- My repos (`ezalos/*`): commit straight to the default branch and push right after —
  other machines pull from the remote, never from this one. Other repos: their contribution workflow
- Never `--no-verify` / `--no-hooks`; when a hook fails: read, fix, re-run
- Commit messages containing em-dashes/apostrophes/smart-quotes: Write to a temp
  file, then `git commit -F` <!-- bash mangles them in heredocs, multiple incidents; <<'EOF' does not save you -->
## Code
- Never rewrite working code from scratch, never make unrelated changes
- Never remove comments unless provably false — comments are documentation
- No 'improved/new/enhanced' names — evergreen only
- Code files open with a 2-line `ABOUTME:` comment
- Never `rm` — always `rip` (graveyard; `rip --unbury` recovers). Beware: rip on a
  symlink to a directory tries to bury the TARGET; use `unlink` for symlinks
- Headless Chrome: NEVER a fixed/derived `--remote-debugging-port` (9222, `9333+W`,
  …). A CDP port on loopback is reachable by ANY local uid, and CDP + `file://` =
  arbitrary file read AS the chrome's user — on a shared machine that hands another local user my SSH
  keys.
  Use `--remote-debugging-pipe` (no TCP), or `--remote-debugging-port=0` and read
  the chosen port from `<user-data-dir>/DevToolsActivePort`. Always tear the browser
  down in a `finally`/`trap` so a hung run can't leave the port open for hours
## Testing
- Test failures are your responsibility; never delete a failing test — raise it with me
- Never write tests that only exercise mocks; test output must be pristine

# The stack (every repo)
- Python entry points: `uv run`, never bare `python`
- Config: direnv + gitignored 0600 `.envrc`; commit an `.envrc.example`
- Secrets: Proton vault, appearing ONLY as `pass://` refs in `.envrc`. The taxonomy
  test: would leaking it grant access or cost money? Never echo a secret value;
  vault writes are Louis-only. Wiring or diagnosing one: `secrets` skill.
  Non-interactive shells (agent Bash, cron): `secrets run -- <cmd>`

